// The one generic sum, over anything that adds and has a zero to start from.
//
// This is what stayed behind when the complex arithmetic moved into `sysl.math.complex`. It is here
// rather than there because it was never about complex numbers: it is about a bound that promises
// *behaviour* and never a *value*, which is the reason `sum` could not be written at all before a
// trait member was allowed to have no receiver.
//
// A `total[T: Add](xs: []T) -> T` cannot start its accumulation. `Add` promises how to add two `T`s
// and nothing promises a zero, so the sum has to be seeded from `xs[0]` — which makes an empty input
// a different question and puts an `Option` in the signature of the most ordinary function there is.
//
// **The finding is discharged, and this file is what is left of it.** It used to declare a trait of
// its own — `Additive`, with an `identity()` and three `impl` blocks — because the core `Zero`
// covered the floats and `Complex` and could not cover `int`: membership for the open `iN` / `uN`
// families has to be the compiler's, and every membership the compiler handed out was a method with
// a **receiver**, which a `zero()` has no value to be called on. A provided membership may now have
// no receiver, so every integer width is a member of `Zero` and `[T: Add + Zero]` spans the whole
// selection this program has. The trait, the blocks and the free name they needed are all gone.
//
// **What the trait cost while it existed is worth keeping, because the next program to write one
// will pay it too.** Its member had to be called `identity` rather than the obvious `zero`: a
// trait's members become the implementing type's, and `zero` on `Complex` was already taken — by
// the core `Zero`, the very trait `Additive` was a wider version of. A trait a program writes for
// types it did not write has to pick names still free on all of them, and the more ordinary the
// concept the likelier the collision.

// The sum that starts at the type's own zero, so an empty sequence has the answer every other
// sequence has, and one body serves a built-in width and a library type alike.
//
// **`T.zero()` carries the type's own arguments.** At `T = Complex[real]` the member reached is
// `Complex`'s, and its `F` is the `real` the parameter arrived applied to — so nothing has to be
// annotated for the expected type to settle it, which is what the `var total: T` written here used
// to be doing.
sum[T: Add + Zero](xs: []const T) -> T
    var total = T.zero()

    for i in 0..<xs.len do total += xs[i]

    total
end sum
